    "message": "feat(websearch): correct adapter parameters and document every option\n\nAudited all 14 adapters against their providers' current API references.\n\nLinkup never sent the documented maxResults, so every call returned the\nprovider's default set and paid for results the client-side slice then threw\naway. Its catalog also omitted the cheapest `fast` depth, and the full extracted\npage text it returns under `content` was dropped instead of being carried as\nitem content. Added the documented fromDate/toDate range.\n\nParallel targeted /v1beta/search while the catalog exposed v1's mode vocabulary\n(turbo/basic/advanced); the beta endpoint accepts fast/one-shot/agentic, so every\nconfigured mode was invalid for the endpoint actually called. Migrated to\n/v1/search, which is what the catalog already described: mode and max_chars_total\nstay top level, max_results and excerpt_settings move under advanced_settings,\nand source_policy makes domain filtering real -- SUPPORTS_DOMAINS was False\ndespite include_domains/exclude_domains being documented, so base.py was zeroing\nthe filters before the adapter saw them.\n\nFirecrawl's limit is per source type, so requesting max_results from each of N\nsources fetched and billed N times what the caller could use; it is now divided\nacross the configured sources. Added the country option, since Firecrawl\nsilently defaults to US, and categories (github/research/pdf).\n\nTavily gained chunks_per_source, country and start/end date, and now clamps\nmax_results to the documented 0-20 rather than letting the provider reject it.\n\nSafe search was reachable on 1 of 14 providers despite being documented by six.\nWired it for Brave, SearXNG, SerpAPI and SearchAPI.\n\nFinally, 37 of 53 options had no cost_note, so the admin UI rendered the generic\n\"Dotenv-only advanced option.\" string -- telling an operator nothing about what\nleaving the field blank does. All 66 options now state their empty behaviour,\nwith a contract test to keep it that way.\n\nDeliberately not mapped: Firecrawl creditsUsed, Tavily include_usage and\nParallel usage report credits and SKU counts, not currency. Converting them to\ncost_usd would need a hardcoded price table that goes stale silently, so cost\nstays populated only where a provider reports actual dollars (Exa).\n\nVerification: new tests assert maxResults is sent, Linkup's date range,\nParallel's source_policy nesting and v1 endpoint, Tavily's clamp, Firecrawl's\nper-source division, and safe search reaching three providers; the catalog\ncontract table was regenerated. Full suite passes except 14 failures that also\nfail on unmodified main -- 8 from real API keys in the local environment\ndefeating test isolation, 6 from the PowerShell installer tests.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>",
